  "title": "A Classical-Mass-Action Sensitivity Zero in an Exact Six-Complex Census",
  "batch_label": "78",
  "kind": "resolution-paper",
  "original_status": "proved",
  "scope": "For the ordered complexes (0,X,Y,2X,X+Y,2Y), among all simple directed networks with three or four reactions satisfying rank(S)=2, existence of a strictly positive steady flux, and nonzero independent-symbol det(SR), there does exist a generic-nonzero inverse sensitivity that becomes identically zero under classical mass action on Sv=0. In the canonical species-swap order and the reaction-then-species order fixed in problem.md, the lexicographically first triple is E=(0->X, 0->2X, X->Y, X+Y->0), reaction rho=0->X, species m=Y. Its generic signed cofactor is -r_(X->Y),X+r_(X+Y->0),X, while its mass-action specialization is -v_(X->Y)+v_(X+Y->0)=-(Sv)_Y; the mass-action determinant is nonzero on the positive steady-state locus (for example it equals 8 at v=(2,1,2,2) with the recorded Gale basis).",
